  "comment": "The geometry parity fixture (spec/world.md, 'The geometry of the shapes'): for one instance of every parametric shape, the exact axis-aligned bounds its mesh must have (`bounds`, min and max per axis), the format's conservative half-extents (`half_extents`, what Shape::local_aabb_half returns — the mesh fits inside), and `landmarks`: points that must be vertices of the mesh, within `tolerance` metres. Landmarks pin what dimensions alone cannot — which way a wedge faces, where a pyramid's apex is, the plane a torus lies in, a prism's first vertex, a polyhedron's orientation — and leave the phase of a ring around an axis of revolution to the renderer (no landmark sits on a cylinder's rim). Any renderer can run it: build each shape, check the bounds, find each landmark among the vertices. The JS reference renderer runs it in CI (js/test/geometry.test.mjs); the Rust reference checks the half-extents (rust/tests/geometry.rs). The instances are the ones conformance/shapes.json and conformance/prism.json draw.",
